/-!
# Endpoint dominance on the fiber product of canonical tuple loci

For tuples `x`, `y`, `z` in a field `Ω` over `k`, let `P` be the actual scheme fiber product of
`Spec k[x, y]` and `Spec k[y, z]` over `Spec k[y]`, with its actual projections `πX`, `πY`, `πZ`
to `Spec k[x]`, `Spec k[y]`, `Spec k[z]`.  Suppose every coordinate of `x` is algebraic over the
field generated by `y` and every coordinate of `y` over the field generated by `x`, and likewise
for `y` and `z` (the algebraicity clauses of (8.1)).  Then any subset
of `P` has dense image under `πY` if and only if it has dense image under `πX`, and likewise for
`πZ` (`locusPullback_endDominance`).  With `locusPullback_dominatingComponents` this makes the
components dominating the middle base exactly those dominating both ends.

The proof pushes the subset to `Spec k[x, y]` (resp. `Spec k[y, z]`) and reflects density along
the injective algebraic extensions `k[y] → k[x, y] ← k[x]`; the algebraicity is derived from the
closure hypotheses.  No component classification is used.  Both steps are public
(`denseImage_iff_of_isAlgebraic`, `isAlgebraic_adjoin_of_subset_racl`); `LocusQuotientComponents`
also applies them to the literal quotient spectrum.

This module is part of the formalization of the Evans–Hrushovski–Gismatullin
reconstruction theorem; the source of truth is `sources/blueprint.tex`.

**Status:** proved (#5, C2 support).
-/


